Jonadab the Unsightly One wrote:
> Doug Freyburger <dfrey...@yahoo.com> wrote:
>
>> Wizards start out very vulnerable
>
> I noticed.

How tp play a wizard evolves through several very different phases
across the game.  Early on it's a matter of evading melee while managing
to trade experience points with your pet.

>> Weapons - The starting quarterstaff is a trap to get
>> you to waste skill slots.
>
> I sort of figured that (rather like the Tourist's darts:
> you're not going to be continuing to use that skill for
> very long), but at this point I don't think I've managed
> to get a wizard to survive long enough to "feel more
> confident" with it anyway, so I haven't had to make
> the decision to spend a skill slot or not.

The earlier in the game the more important it is to evade melee as
completely as possible.  If you survive to be offered a slot in
quarterstaff you are playing too much melee and that's why you're dying
so often.

>> First elven dagger that does not stack, figure it's
>> enchanted so make it Sting.  Only use it for melee
>> in an emergency.
>
> Wait, what?  WHY would I turn a dagger into Sting
> if I don't intend to use Sting?

It's not as against the grain as it might seem.  You'll want to avoid
melee but you aren't going to be able to avoid it completely.  May as
well make your hits count towards the high priority skill enhancement of
dagger to get the most multifire.  Plus you'll remove the temptation to
fight melee with the quarterstaff.

The next reason is orcs are very common in the early game.  May as well
cause them double damage.  Sting will often kill an orc on a single
thrown hit.

>But still, creating an artifact
> you have no intention of using seems odd to me.
>
> (Chaotic wizards, of course, can usefully wish for
> the Master Key of Half Physical Damage and so
> have a definite vested interest in keeping artifacts
> to a minimum until they get the castle wand.  It is
> worth creating Magicbane because of the obvious
> benefits, but for a chaotic wizard to deliberately
> create Sting would be actively counterproductive.)

Our difference - Wishing for artifacts seems odd to me.  I don't wish
for Quest artifacts at all to keep classes as diffreent as I can.  For
wizards I rarely wish for artifacts at all. Once I get hungerless cast
of create monster I can do a sacrifest and end up with lots of
artifacts.

>> Wear [mithril] and play as a dagger thrower...
>> when you build confidence that you can survive
>> by running and firing, switch to it.  Then you'll be
>> able to run, cast and fire.
>
> That part all makes sense, but I'm having trouble
> rather earlier in the game.  My wizards have been
> dying before finding any body armor at all, let alone
> mithril, before finding more than one or two crude
> daggers at most, sometimes before finding the
> entrance to the mines.  I have similar problems
> with healers.

You've played a lot of combat wombat classes?  Early wizards live by
evasion.  Late wizards eventually evolve into tanks.  Extremely early in
the game wizards evade melee, cast force bolt, use their pet to kill
monsters.  Then they become mithril armored dagger throwers so more like
rangers.  Then as elven leather armor comes available they can have
enough protection to switch to studded leather armor to fire and cast. 
After the Castle they end up dragon scale mail armored tanks.


